<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (We opted for two kickstands mounted on the outer perimeter of the chassis. By distributing the weight of the robot across both, we could reduce strain to any singular servo. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (When we were making changes for iteration two of the bot, we decided to add a kickstand for endgame play. With our robot using all 8 motor slots we decided that the kick stand, only requiring a servo, was the best option without having to redesign our other systems. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (Each kickstand consists of a large gear with a flat beam, and a small wheel placed on the bottom to allow it to roll This also allows for the drivers to adjust the park easier. After Meet 2, we decided to flip the placement of the small wheel from facing out, to facing in to allow us to fit better within our 18 by 18 inch guidelines. We also placed a rod to prevent the servo from zeroing to the wrong location to ensure that the system always starts in the correct position.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (Our choice of actuator at first was a speed servo, however we quickly realized that we needed more speed on our intake. We ended up going through a super speed servo, a 312 RPM motor, and then a 435 RPM motor, which finally ended up giving us the speed that we needed. Since we increased the speed on our intake, we also realized we could slant the ramp on our intake to be steeper.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
